{"name":"Brooch Ivory","hex":"#f3e2c3","fetched":"2026-10-18","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"brooch","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A piece of ornamental jewellery having a pin allowing it to be fixed to garments worn on the upper body.","A painting all of one colour, such as a sepia painting."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To adorn as with a brooch."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brooch"},{"term":"ivory","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The hard white form of dentin which forms the tusks of elephants, walruses and other animals.","A creamy white color, the color of ivory."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of ivory.","Resembling or having the colour of ivory."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ivory"}],"entries":[{"title":"Brooch","blocks":[{"text":"A brooch is a decorative jewellery item, or the insignia of a fraternal order, designed to be attached to garments, often to fasten them together. It is usually made of metal, often silver or gold or some other material. Brooches are frequently decorated with enamel or with gemstones and may be solely for ornament or serve a practical function as a clothes fastener. The earliest known brooches are from the Bronze Age. As fashions in brooches changed rather quickly, they are important chronological indicators. In archaeology, ancient European brooches are usually referred to by the Latin term fibula. One example is the Tara Brooch."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Wing_Brooch_MET_DT108.jpg/330px-Wing_Brooch_MET_DT108.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brooch","wikidata":"Q499916"},{"title":"Ivory","blocks":[{"text":"Ivory is a hard, white material from the tusks and teeth of animals, that consists mainly of dentin, one of the physical structures of teeth and tusks. The chemical structure of the teeth and tusks of mammals is the same, regardless of the species of origin, but ivory contains structures of mineralized collagen. The trade in certain tusks and teeth other than elephant is well established and widespread; therefore, \"ivory\" can correctly be used to describe any mammalian teeth or tusks of commercial interest which are large enough to be carved or scrimshawed."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Horn_Louvre_OA4069.jpg/330px-Horn_Louvre_OA4069.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ivory","wikidata":"Q82001"},{"title":"Ivory (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Ivory is an off-white color named after, and derived from, the material made from the tusks and teeth of certain animals, such as the elephant and the walrus. It has a very slight tint of yellow."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Decorated_ivory.JPG/330px-Decorated_ivory.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ivory_(color)","wikidata":"Q1358981"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Wing_Brooch_MET_DT108.jpg/500px-Wing_Brooch_MET_DT108.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Wing_Brooch_MET_DT108.jpg/1280px-Wing_Brooch_MET_DT108.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Wing_Brooch_MET_DT108.jpg/500px-Wing_Brooch_MET_DT108.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wing Brooch, 2nd century AD, Metropolitan Museum of Art","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWing_Brooch_MET_DT108.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Skederids_kyrka_interi%C3%B6r%2C_f%C3%B6rs%C3%A4ljningsbord.jpg/500px-Skederids_kyrka_interi%C3%B6r%2C_